L001957	\section{V. Comparation of quantum memories}
L001958	{We use QIR to quantify the performance of our quantum memory as well as the others in this section. We suppose that only depolarizing noise induces storage infidelity, and most of quantum memories have tested their performance of storing photonic qubits with fidelity $F_s$, therefore, the depolarizing noise is estimated by the photonic qubit storage fidelity, which follows $F_s=1-p_n/2$. The channel capacity $C_{qm}$ can be estimated following Eq.~\ref{eqCQM}, and combining the distribution time $T_{tot}$ (Eq.~3 in main article) we can calcultate QIR by,
L001959	\begin{equation}
L001960	\label{eqRqm}
L001961	\begin{aligned}
L001962	 \mathcal{R}_{qm} = C_{qm}/T_{tot}.
L001963	\end{aligned}
L001964	\end{equation}}
L001965	
L001966	Storage lifetime is as well a critical criterion for a quantum memory. For a realistic memory with finite lifetime $\tau$ (in unit of second), the average entanglement distribution time becomes~\cite{PhysRevLett.98.060502},
L001967	\begin{equation}
L001968	\label{eqTtau}
L001969	\begin{aligned}
L001970	T_{\tau}=\frac{T_{tot}-\frac{1+p_e}{p_ep_s}\frac{q^{\tau+1}}{1-p_e/2}}{1-\frac{q^{\tau+1}}{1-p_e/2}},
L001971	\end{aligned}
L001972	\end{equation}
L001973	where $p_s$ is probability of a successful entanglement swapping within a repeater node, $p_e$ is the probabilty to entangle adjacent memories, and $q=1-p_e$. After considering the storage time, the comprehensive performance could be modifies as,
L001974	\begin{equation}
L001975	\label{eqRt}
L001976	\begin{aligned}
L001977	 \mathcal{R}_{\tau} = C_{qm}/T_{\tau}.
L001978	\end{aligned}
L001979	\end{equation}
L001980	
L001981	
L001982	
L001983	In Table~\ref{tab1}, we benchmark the performance of our quantum memory, as well as other state-of-the-art multimode/multipartite quantum memories, using the criterion $\mathcal{R}_{qm}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{\tau}$. The following ideal assumptions are made in the comparison:
L001984	(i) the total length of quantum repeater channel is 1000~km and the segement length is 250~km;
L001985	(ii) In all cases, storage efficiency refers to the intrinsic retrieval efficiency;
L001986	(iii) multiplexing is counted only when each mode can be individually controlled or detected. Otherwise, modes are treated as degrees of freedom for a high-dimensional communication channel;
L001987	(iv) the dominant error is assumed to arise from crosstalk between neighboring modes which is modeled as depolarizing noise;
L001988	(v) all modes are equally likely, i.e., $p(x)=1/M$ for $x=1,\dots,M$.
L001989	
L001990	Under these conditions, our quantum memory outperforms others primarily due to its exceptionally high storage efficiency and fidelity, combined with intermediate multimode capacity. Its performance could be further enhanced by integrating additional multiplexing modes. Notably, the channel capacity increases slower with larger mode number when the storage fidelity is not sufficiently high~\cite{PhysRevLett.118.110501, PhysRevA.101.032312}. This highlights that, beyond merely increasing multimode capacity, high fidelity is also a decisive factor in achieving superior comprehensive performance.
